## `fmt::format("{}", x)` — end-to-end trace

### 1. Entry point: `fmt::format`
`include/fmt/format.h:4362-4366`
```cpp
template <typename... T>
FMT_NODISCARD FMT_INLINE auto format(format_string<T...> fmt, T&&... args) -> std::string {
  return vformat(fmt, fmt::make_format_args(args...));
}
```
- The format string's type is `format_string<T...>` (`include/fmt/base.h:2902`), a wrapper around `basic_format_string<char, T...>` that compile-time-parses `"{}"` against the argument-type pack `T...` and reports a **compile error** for mismatched placeholders — the actual runtime string it holds is just a `string_view`.
- `x`'s type is deduced as `T`, and the call immediately does two things: type-erases the arguments, then forwards to `vformat`.

### 2. Type erasure: `make_format_args` → `format_arg_store` → `basic_format_args`
`include/fmt/base.h:2016-2020` builds a `detail::format_arg_store<Context, NUM_ARGS, 0, DESC>` (defined `base.h:1682-1719`), whose constructor calls `detail::make_arg<...>(values)...` for every argument (`base.h:1620` and `1651`). `make_arg` copies/references `x` into a `detail::value<Context>` union member and records a `detail::type` tag (e.g. `int_type`, `string_type`, or `custom_type` for user-defined types) — this pairing is exactly one `basic_format_arg<Context>` (`base.h:1726`, a hand-rolled tagged union, not `std::variant`, to keep codegen small).
- For a `custom_type`, `make_arg` additionally stores a type-erased function pointer that knows how to call back into `T`'s `formatter<T>` — this is the hook that lets user-defined types plug into the system (see step 5).
- `format_arg_store` implicitly converts to `basic_format_args<Context>` via the constructor at `base.h:1889-1892` — this is the actual `format_args` (an alias for `basic_format_args<format_context>`) passed into `vformat`. This object holds only a descriptor bitmask (`desc_`) plus a pointer into the `format_arg_store`'s array — no template parameter on the argument types survives past this point, which is the "type erasure."

### 3. `vformat` — allocate a buffer, delegate to `vformat_to`, stringify
`include/fmt/format-inl.h:1431-1437`
```cpp
FMT_FUNC auto vformat(string_view fmt, format_args args) -> std::string {
  auto buffer = memory_buffer();
  detail::vformat_to(buffer, fmt, args);
  return to_string(buffer);
}
```
`memory_buffer` is `basic_memory_buffer<char>` (`include/fmt/format.h:835-909`) — a `detail::buffer<char>` (`include/fmt/base.h:852-948`) that starts backed by a small inline stack array (`store_[SIZE]`) and grows via a static `grow` callback (`format.h:848-870`) that reallocates at ~1.5× capacity (falling back to the requested size, capped by the allocator's `max_size`) whenever `push_back`/`try_reserve` needs more room than is currently available. This is the "memory buffer" all writing happens into — no heap allocation at all for short results that fit the inline array.

### 4. Routing through `vformat_to` — the fast path for exactly `"{}"`
`include/fmt/format.h:4220-4286` is where the real work happens:
```cpp
void vformat_to(buffer<Char>& buf, basic_string_view<Char> fmt,
                typename vformat_args<Char>::type args, locale_ref loc) {
  auto out = basic_appender<Char>(buf);
  if (fmt.size() == 2 && equal2(fmt.data(), "{}")) {
    auto arg = args.get(0);
    if (!arg) report_error("argument not found");
    arg.visit(default_arg_formatter<Char>{out, args, loc});
    return;
  }
  ... // general parse_format_string path for anything more complex
}
```
`fmt::format("{}", x)` is *literally* the special-cased branch fmt carries specifically to keep the common case cheap: it skips the format-string parser entirely, fetches the single argument (`args.get(0)`), and dispatches it straight to a visitor. `basic_appender<Char>` (`base.h:1154-1180`) is the output iterator: assigning to it calls `buffer_->push_back(c)`, i.e. writes land directly in the `memory_buffer` from step 3.

For anything beyond a bare `"{}"`, the `else` path builds a `format_handler` combining a `basic_format_parse_context` and a `buffered_context` (`= basic_format_context<basic_appender<Char>, Char>`, aliased at `base.h:1992`), then drives `detail::parse_format_string<false>(fmt, format_handler(...))`, which scans the string and calls back `on_text`/`on_arg_id`/`on_replacement_field`/`on_format_specs` as it goes — the latter parses per-argument `{:...}` specs into `dynamic_format_specs` and visits with `arg_formatter<Char>` instead of `default_arg_formatter`.

### 5. Dispatch by type — `basic_format_arg::visit` and `formatter<T>` specializations
`basic_format_arg<Context>::visit` (`base.h:1779-1816`) is a `switch` over the stored `type` tag that calls `vis(value)` with the concrete alternative unpacked — this is where type erasure is reversed just long enough to call the right code:
- **Built-in/fundamental types** (`int_type`, `double_type`, `string_type`, …): `vis` is `default_arg_formatter::operator()(T value)` (`format.h:3726-3728`), which calls the free function `write<Char>(out, value)` — an overload set in `format.h` that formats ints/floats/strings/chars/bools directly and efficiently, bypassing any `formatter` object.
- **Custom/user-defined types** (`custom_type`): `vis` gets a `basic_format_arg<Context>::handle` wrapping the object pointer plus the type-erased callback captured back in `make_arg`. That callback (`default_arg_formatter::operator()(handle)`, `format.h:3729-3734`) constructs `Context::template formatter_type<T>` — i.e. the **`fmt::formatter<T>` specialization** the user wrote or fmt provides (primary template deleted at `base.h:1140-1144` to force an explicit specialization) — calls its `parse()` on an empty parse context, then `format(value, ctx)`, writing through `ctx.out()` straight into the same buffer-backed appender. The generic `write<...>(OutputIt, const T&)` helper at `format.h:3702-3714` shows the same three-step shape (`formatter.parse` → `formatter.format` → return iterator) used whenever a `formatter<T>` is invoked, whether from the `"{}"` fast path or the general spec-parsing path (`on_format_specs`/`arg.format_custom`, `format.h:4268` and `4278-4279`).

So for `x` an `int`/`std::string`/etc., the value is written via `write<Char>` with zero `formatter` indirection; for a user-defined type with a `formatter<T>` specialization, that specialization's `format()` method is what actually appends characters — this is the extension point the whole library is built around.

### 6. Buffer → `std::string`
Back in `vformat` (`format-inl.h:1436`), once `vformat_to` returns, `to_string(buffer)` (`format.h:4202-4207`) does the final conversion:
```cpp
template <typename Char, size_t SIZE>
auto to_string(const basic_memory_buffer<Char, SIZE>& buf) -> std::basic_string<Char> {
  auto size = buf.size();
  return std::basic_string<Char>(buf.data(), size);
}
```
This makes exactly one owning copy from the buffer's internal storage (inline array or grown heap block) into the returned `std::string`; the `memory_buffer` itself (and any heap allocation it made) is destroyed when `vformat` returns.

### Summary of the pipeline
```
fmt::format("{}", x)
  → make_format_args(x)              [base.h:2016]  type-erase x into basic_format_arg → basic_format_args
  → vformat(fmt, args)                [format-inl.h:1431]  allocate memory_buffer
    → detail::vformat_to(buffer, fmt, args)   [format.h:4220]  "{}" fast path (or parse_format_string general path)
      → arg.visit(default_arg_formatter{...})  [base.h:1779]  switch on type tag
        → write<Char>(out, value)              [format.h ~3700s] for built-ins
        → formatter<T>::parse()+format()       [user specialization]  for custom types
          → basic_appender → buffer.push_back  [base.h:1154 / 852]  grows buffer as needed
  → to_string(buffer)                 [format.h:4202]  copy buffer contents into std::string
```
